can check if it's still a leaf and removable and utility <= threshold. Also, we must ensure we don't get infinite loops: we only enqueue parent after removal, and only when we actually remove a child. The parent's utility might be high, but after removal of a child, its utility does not change. So the parent will only be removed if it becomes a leaf and meets the utility threshold. That's fine.\n\nWe must also consider that the queue might have nodes that are not leaves but we skip them. We must not remove non-leaves. That's fine.\n\nNow, output: retained_node_ids: all ids that are not in removed set. visit_order: list of node ids in the order they were popped from the queue (including skipped? The description says \"complete visit order\". I think it means the order in which we processed each node (pop from queue). Even if we skip it because it's already removed, we still visited it. But maybe we should include only when we actually consider it? The term \"visit\" could mean when we check it. The problem says \"Skip nodes already removed.\" So they are still visited but skipped. The \"complete visit order\" probably includes all nodes popped from the queue. However, it might also include enqueuing parent after removal? That parent is not yet visited until popped. So we retain the order of pop.\n\nBut careful: In the initial queue, we have all nodes. Some nodes might be non-leaves, and we will pop them and skip because they are not removable leaves. But they are visited. So visit_order will include them. Then later when we remove a child and enqueue parent, that parent might have been visited earlier (e.g., a node n2 popped at some point, skipped because it had children; later a child is removed, we enqueue n2 again; when we pop n2 again, it's visited again. That's fine. So visit_order may have duplicates? That seems plausible. The visit order records each time we pop a node from the queue. So the same node could appear multiple times if it was enqueued multiple tim