    - parent_id = node[\"parent\"]\n    - if parent_id is not None and parent_id not in removed:\n      - heapq.heappush(heap, (-nodes_dict[parent_id][\"depth\"], parent_id))\n- After loop, retained_node_ids = [node[\"id\"] for node in nodes if node[\"id\"] not in removed]\n- Sort retained_node_ids? The output schema says \"retained_node_ids\" is an array of strings; no ordering specified. Maybe keep in original order? But to be deterministic, we can keep in order of node id? The fixture doesn't specify. We'll just collect in the order they appear in the input nodes list (or sorted by id). I'll keep in the order of input for simplicity.\n- Return {\"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids, \"visit_order\": visit_order}\n\nBut careful: The visit_order should include the order we popped nodes. We are popping from heap, which gives depth descend and id ascending. That is the visit_order.\n\nPotential issue: When we enqueue parent, we might be adding it again even if it's already in the heap. That's fine; we'll handle duplicates by checking removed status when popped. Also, we might add a parent that is already removed? We check parent not in removed, so that's safe.\n\nBut we need to ensure that we don't keep pushing the same parent repeatedly if multiple children are removed. That will cause multiple entries, but that's acceptable as they will all be processed and skipped after the first removal? Actually, after a child is removed, we push parent. Later, if another child is removed, we push parent again. The parent may have been visited already and possibly removed? But parent removal only possible if it becomes leaf and meets threshold. If parent not removed, it's still not removed, so it will be enqueued again. That's fine. The queue will process it again, and if it's still a leaf (after all children removed) and meets threshold, it will be removed. So algorithm works.\n\nNow test with given fixtures: We can mentally run one case to see if output matches expected? There is no expected output provided. We need to generate code that likely passes hidden tests. We must implement exactly as described.\n\nOne nuance: The problem says \"Remove only a removable leaf whose utility is at most the threshold.\" So utility <= threshold (not strictly less). Also \"After removal enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration.\" So we enqueue the parent. But we should enqueue the parent only if it is retained (i.e., not removed). That's already done.\n\nNow, consider the initial queue: Should we include root? Root depth 0, utility 1, removable false. Root will be popped when all deeper nodes are processed. Root is not a leaf (unless all nodes removed). But root not removable, so it will never be removed. But it should be visited. So yes, include.\n\nNow, about \"skip nodes already removed\": When we pop a node that is already removed, we skip. But we still append to visit_order. That is correct.\n\nNow, about \"no_invalidated_revisit\": This contract might mean we should not revisit a node that has been invalidated (removed) unless it is re-enqueued? We are skipping removed nodes, so we avoid invalid revisits. But we do revisit parents after children removed, which is intentional.\n\nNow, about \"normalized_output\": retained_node_ids and visit_order as defined.\n\nNow, about \"termination\": algorithm should terminate because each removal reduces the number of nodes, and we only enqueue parents, which eventually either get removed or not. No infinite loops.\n\nNow, implementing in Python. We'll use import heapq at top.\n\nWe need to output code as a JSON string with key \"implementation\". The code must define solve(case). We'll write it accordingly.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The payload may have a \"_case_nonce\" field, but we ignore it. The nodes may be in any order. We'll build dict and children list.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nWe need to be careful about the heap ordering: we push (-depth, id). depth is integer, id is string. heapq compares tuples elementwise. That will sort by -depth descending, and for same -depth (i.e., same depth), it compares id ascending. That's correct.\n\nNow, we need to ensure that we handle cases where parent may have multiple children. We'll compute leaf status correctly.\n\nNow, output JSON: we must return a dict with keys \"retained_node_ids\" and \"visit_order\". The values are lists of strings. Ensure they are JSON-serializable (Python lists of strings are fine).\n\nNow, we must format the response as one JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value being the escaped Python source string. We'll write the code with proper indentation, then escape for JSON.\n\nLet me write the code:\n\n```python\nimport heapq\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\n    \n    # Build mappings\n    node_map = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\n    children = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        parent = node.get('parent')\n        if parent is not None:\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(node['id'])\n    \n    removed = set()\n    # Initial heap: all nodes, order by descending depth, then ascending id\n    heap = []\n    for node in nodes:\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (-node['depth'], node['id']))\n    \n    visit_order = []\n    \n    while heap:\n        neg_depth, nid = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        visit_order.append(nid)\n        if nid in removed:\n            continue\n        node = node_map[nid]\n        # Check if leaf: no children still present\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\n        is_leaf = all(cid in removed for cid in child_ids)\n        if is_leaf and node['removable'] and node['utility'] <= threshold:\n            # Remove this node\n            removed.add(nid)\n            # Enqueue parent for reconsideration\n            parent = node.get('parent')\n            if parent is not None and parent not in removed:\n                parent_node = node_map[parent]\n                heapq.heappush(heap, (-parent_node['depth'], parent))\n    \n    retained = [node['id'] for node in nodes if node['id'] not in removed]\n    return {\n        'retained_node_ids': retained,\n        'visit_order': visit_order\n    }\n```\n\nBut we need to be careful: In the initial heap, we push all nodes. However, the heap order may not strictly preserve the original \"stable node-ID order\" for the queue order because we push them in arbitrary order, but heap will order them correctly as we pop. That's fine.\n\nBut consider that the heap might not give deterministic order if we push nodes with same depth and same id? That's impossible because id is unique.\n\nNow, we need to ensure that we don't accidentally include a node multiple times in `retained`? We just filter nodes list.\n\nNow, about the output contract: `retained_node_ids` must be an array of strings. We use original order? Might be okay. Could be required to be sorted? Not specified. We'll keep it as we built.\n\nOne more thing: The problem says \"Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\" The retained IDs could be in any order, but likely sorted by id or depth? Not specified. We'll just output in order of original nodes list.\n\nNow, test with the first fixture case mentally? We can quickly simulate but not necessary.\n\nI think this implementation satisfies the description.\n\nNow, we need to output JSON with key \"implementation\". So we must serialize the code as a JSON string.\n\nWe'll write:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"import heapq\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\\n    node_map = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\\n    children = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(node['id'])\\n    removed = set()\\n    heap = []\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (-node['depth'], node['id']))\\n    visit_order = []\\n    while heap:\\n        neg_depth, nid = heapq.heappop(heap)\\n        visit_order.append(nid)\\n        if nid in removed:\\n            continue\\n        node = node_map[nid]\\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\\n        is_leaf = all(cid in removed for cid in child_ids)\\n        if is_leaf and node['removable'] and node['utility'] <= threshold:\\n            removed.add(nid)\\n            parent = node.get('parent')\\n            if parent is not None and parent not in removed:\\n                parent_node = node_map[parent]\\n                heapq.heappush(heap, (-parent_node['depth'], parent))\\n    retained = [node['id'] for node in nodes if node['id'] not in removed]\\n    return {\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained,\\n        'visit_order': visit_order\\n    }\"\n}\n```\n\nBut we need to escape backslashes and quotes properly. The code above contains single quotes and double quotes? The code uses single quotes for dictionary keys else? In the code, we have single quotes for dict keys like 'nodes', 'utility_threshold', etc. That's fine; no double quotes. But we need to ensure the JSON string is properly escaped: backslashes in newline \\n, and double quotes if any. In the code, we have double quotes only in 'removable'? No, all strings use single quotes. So we just need to escape backslashes: each newline becomes \\n, each tab becomes \\t. We'll just write as a string in our final JSON. We'll be careful.\n\nWe can also note that we import heapq at top. That's fine.\n\nThus final response is that JSON.\n\nWe should also ensure that the code does not mutate case; we only read.\n\nNow produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2018,"completion_tokens":4821,"total_tokens":6839,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1920},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4471},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1920,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":98},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8